b:["$","section",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-semibold","children":"The 10 Transformation Domains"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Each domain maps to established constructs in the peer-reviewed NDE literature."}]]}]
c:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"1. 🌿 Appreciation for Life (AL)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in how much the experiencer values being alive, notices beauty, savors ordinary moments, and feels gratitude for existence."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Consistently identified as one of the strongest and most universal aftereffects (Ring, 1984; Greyson LCI-R; van Lommel, 2010; Long/NDERF). NDErs frequently describe the world as more vivid, beautiful, and precious after their experience."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↑ Increase"}]]}]]}]
d:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"2. 🪞 Self-Perception & Identity (SI)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in how the experiencer sees themselves—self-worth, self-acceptance, inner peace, confidence, personality traits, and sense of who they are."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Ring (1984) documented increased self-acceptance and inner peace. Greyson's LCI-R includes a \"Self-Acceptance\" factor. Atwater (2007) noted personality changes including increased assertiveness and emotional sensitivity. Many experiencers report feeling like a \"different person\" after their NDE."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↑ Self-acceptance increases; identity may be disrupted then reconstructed"}]]}]]}]
e:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"3. 💛 Compassion & Concern for Others (CC)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in empathy, care for others, desire to help or serve, tolerance, and the capacity for unconditional love."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Among the most consistently reported aftereffects across all studies (Ring, 1984; Greyson LCI-R; van Lommel, 2010; Sutherland, 1992; Long/NDERF). NDErs frequently report heightened empathy—sometimes to the point of feeling others' emotions or pain—and an overwhelming desire to be of service."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↑ Increase"}]]}]]}]
f:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"4. 💎 Values & Priorities (VP)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in what the experiencer considers important in life—particularly shifts regarding materialism, status, competition, wealth, social approval, simplicity, and authenticity."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Decreased materialism and decreased concern with social status are among the most replicated findings in NDE research (Ring, 1984; Greyson LCI-R; van Lommel, 2010; Sutherland, 1992). NDErs consistently report that possessions, money, career prestige, and social image became far less important."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↓ Materialism/status; ↑ Simplicity/authenticity"}]]}]]}]
10:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"5. ✨ Spiritual Awareness (SA)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in the experiencer's sense of connection to something greater than themselves—universal consciousness, the divine, a higher power, oneness, transcendence—and in spiritual practices."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Increased spirituality is one of the most prominent aftereffects (Ring, 1984; Greyson LCI-R; Khanna & Greyson, 2014; van Lommel, 2010). Critically, the research distinguishes between ",["$","em",null,{"children":"spirituality"}]," (personal connection to the transcendent) and ",["$","em",null,{"children":"religiousness"}]," (adherence to organized doctrine). NDErs overwhelmingly report increased spirituality."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↑ Increase"}]]}]]}]
11:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"6. ⛪ Religious Orientation (RO)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in the experiencer's relationship with organized religion—including adherence to specific doctrines, participation in religious institutions, and beliefs about religious exclusivism or universalism."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," This is one of the most nuanced domains. Research consistently shows a complex pattern: NDErs may become more religious, less religious, or—most commonly—shift from organized religion toward personal spirituality (Ring, 1984; van Lommel, 2010; Long/NDERF). The direction is highly variable and important for interpretation."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: Variable — ↑ More religious, ↓ Less religious, → Shift to spirituality/universalism, ↕ Mixed"}]]}]]}]
12:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"7. 🕊️ Attitude Toward Death (AD)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in fear of death, understanding of what death is, belief in an afterlife or continuation of consciousness, and overall peace with mortality."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Reduced fear of death is arguably the single most consistent aftereffect of NDEs, reported across virtually every study (Moody, 1975; Ring, 1980; Greyson, 1992, 2021; van Lommel, 2001; Sutherland, 1992; Long/NDERF). NDErs overwhelmingly describe death as a transition rather than an ending, often reporting complete elimination of death anxiety."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↓ Fear of death; ↑ Belief in continuation"}]]}]]}]
13:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"8. 🔮 Psychic & Expanded Perception (PE)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"The emergence or enhancement of experiences typically classified as psychic or anomalous—including heightened intuition, precognition, telepathy, healing abilities, mediumistic experiences, out-of-body experiences, synchronicities, and electromagnetic sensitivity."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Ring (1984) documented this extensively. Greyson has published multiple studies on paranormal aftereffects. Atwater (1988, 2007) was particularly detailed about EM sensitivity. These experiences are among the most challenging for NDErs because they are often socially stigmatized."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↑ New emergence or significant increase"}]]}]]}]
14:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"9. 🤝 Relationships & Social Dynamics (RS)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in interpersonal relationships—intimate partnerships, family, friendships, and broader social dynamics—as well as the social challenges of being an NDEr."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," Relationship changes are among the most complex and often most painful aftereffects. Research documents both deepening of some relationships and loss of others (Ring, 1984; Sutherland, 1992; Atwater, 2007; Greyson, 2021). Elevated divorce rates among NDErs have been noted. This domain captures both the growth ",["$","em",null,{"children":"and"}]," the struggle."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↕ Mixed — depth increases, but some relationships end due to incompatibility"}]]}]]}]
15:["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-lg border bg-card text-card-foreground shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col space-y-1.5 p-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"font-semibold tracking-tight text-lg","children":"10. 🧭 Purpose, Meaning & Life Direction (PD)"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 pt-0 space-y-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Changes in the experiencer's sense of life purpose, mission, calling, career direction, and quest for knowledge and understanding."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Literature:"}]," A heightened sense of purpose or mission is widely documented (Ring, 1984; van Lommel, 2010; Greyson LCI-R; Atwater, 2007; Long/NDERF). Career changes—often away from high-earning positions toward helping professions—are frequently reported. An intense thirst for knowledge about consciousness, spirituality, and philosophy is common."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-muted-foreground italic","children":"Typical Direction: ↑ Sense of purpose increases; life direction shifts toward service or knowledge"}]]}]]}]
